From ad4b677d9a761f31daed298572201ca50d305afb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 02:56:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/ppc: add AltiVec HEVC deblocking filters, luma and
 chroma, 8 and 10-bit

Deblocking was the one HEVC stage with no PowerPC path at all (neither
upstream nor PowerVLC ever wrote one); MC, SAO and the transforms are
covered, so it was the largest remaining scalar share of a decode on
these machines. New libavcodec/ppc/hevcdsp_deblock_template.c, included
from hevcdsp.c per bit depth like the SAO/inter templates, providing
hevc_{h,v}_loop_filter_{luma,chroma} at 8 and 10 bits and registered in
both the plain and the _c slots (the kernels honour no_p/no_q, so the
pcm/transquant-bypass path deblocking_filter_CTB() takes is served too;
other architectures leave the _c slots on C).

Design: the eight lines of one call are the eight int16 lanes of one
vector per tap p3..q3, lanes 0-3 the first 4-line segment (tc[0],
no_p[0], no_q[0]) and 4-7 the second, so the whole of
hevc_loop_filter_luma() -- the per-segment decisions from lines 0 and 3
(d0 + d3 < beta, the six-part strong test, nd_p/nd_q), the strong and
the weak filter of h2656_deblock_template.c -- is lane arithmetic on
eight vectors. The two lines of a segment are brought together with two
constant permutes (lane 0 and lane 3 of each half broadcast to the
half), strong and weak are both always computed and merged per lane
with vec_sel under the strong/weak/skip and no_p/no_q masks; the only
branch is the early return when neither segment passes d0 + d3 < beta
(or both tc are 0), in which case nothing is stored. The int16
intermediates fit at both depths (largest strong sum 8 * 1023 + 4, weak
delta0 numerator 12 * 1023 + 8); the pixel clip is the store's
vec_packsu at 8 bits and a min/max pair at 10. The horizontal-edge
filters load each tap row directly; the vertical-edge filters load one
row per vector and transpose (8x8 bytes: the vp9dsp network; 8x8 and
8x4 16-bit: three levels of 16-bit merges; 8x4 bytes: the first two
levels of the byte network), filter, and transpose back to store.
Parameters reach the vector unit through one aligned 8 x int16 block
(tc, no_p, no_q per segment, beta) and one vec_ld, written before the
pixel loads so the store has retired by the time it is read.

Store contract as the C, NEON and x86 versions: vertical-edge stores
write the full 8 (chroma: only the p0/q0 pair, two element stores per
row) taps of every row back, p3/q3 unchanged, like the aarch64 version;
horizontal-edge stores touch only rows p2..q2 (chroma p0/q0). No store
reads memory first: a plain vec_st on a 16-aligned 10-bit row,
otherwise a vec_lvsr rotation and stvewx/stvehx/stvebx element stores,
so nothing outside the row's own bytes is rewritten and there is no
read-modify-write anywhere. Loads only touch the aligned 16-byte blocks
holding bytes actually needed (mc_load8_u8's vec_ld(0)/vec_ld(7)
splice, a vec_ld(3) variant for the 4-byte chroma rows), alignment is
recomputed per row, and any pix alignment and stride are handled.
Reuses the inter template's mc_load8/mc_store8/mc_store_mode helpers.

Validated (qemu-ppc-static -cpu 7400, incremental -j1 build,
warning-free): checkasm --test=hevc_deblock seeds 42/1/777 "all 32
tests passed" -- chroma, chroma_full, luma (strong/weak/skip),
luma_full at 8, 10 and 12 bits (12-bit stays C), every 8/10-bit entry
a real ALTIVEC dispatch. Real-G4/G5 --bench pending.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017pZCe229PctajyxMfguX3z
